  t=5/6: gaps all = 1/n? True

n=7:  phi(n) = 6 times form regular n-gon
  t = k/n for k coprime to n: k in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
  t=1/7: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=2/7: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=3/7: gaps all = 1/n? True

n=8:  phi(n) = 4 times form regular n-gon
  t = k/n for k coprime to n: k in [1, 3, 5, 7]
  t=1/8: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=3/8: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=5/8: gaps all = 1/n? True

n=14:  phi(n) = 6 times form regular n-gon
  t = k/n for k coprime to n: k in [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13]
  t=1/14: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=3/14: gaps all = 1/n? True
  t=5/14: gaps all = 1/n? True

KEY INSIGHT: for the initial segment speeds {1,...,n-1},
the lonely times t=k/n (gcd(k,n)=1) are EXACTLY the times
when the runner polygon is a REGULAR n-gon.

The regular polygon is the unique configuration where ALL gaps = 1/n.
LRC for initial segments is the trivial statement that the trajectory
passes through the regular polygon configuration phi(n) times per period.

For NON-initial speeds, the trajectory NEVER forms a perfect regular
polygon — but THM-387 says it must get CLOSE ENOUGH (enter the LL fiber).

======================================================================
PART 4: Twin-Goldbach polygon bridge
======================================================================

Polygon alignment analysis:
For each N, the 'alignment defect' = how far the nearest complement
pair is from being both-twin.

  N=  96: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (19, 77))
  N= 100: REPRESENTABLE (found complement pair)
  N= 200: REPRESENTABLE (found complement pair)
  N= 402: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (59, 343))
  N= 516: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (59, 457))
  N= 786: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (139, 647))
  N=1000: REPRESENTABLE (found complement pair)
  N=1356: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (31, 1325))
  N=3246: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (73, 3173))
  N=4206: EXCEPTION, alignment defect = 4 (nearest miss: (43, 4163))

==================================================
THE BRIDGE: LRC gap defect vs twin-Goldbach alignment defect
==================================================

LRC (THM-387 gap race):
  At each wrap-around, the 'defect' = how far g_right is from 1/n
  when g_left drops below 1/n. Defect 0 = lonely.
  An exception (counterexample) would need ALL wrap-arounds to have
  positive defect. This is like the twin-Goldbach exceptions where
  ALL twin primes have positive alignment defect.

Twin-Goldbach (necklace reduction):
  At each twin prime p, the 'defect' = distance from N-p to the
  nearest twin prime. Defect 0 = representable.
  The 35 exceptions have positive defect at ALL twin primes.

SHARED STRUCTURE:
  1. Both are 'for-all/exists' problems: need ONE good instance
     among many candidates (wrap-arounds / twin primes)
  2. Both have a complement/reflection symmetry
     (t ↔ 1-t for LRC; p ↔ N-p for Goldbach)
  3. Both have finitely many exceptions (conjectured for LRC,
     35 for twin-Goldbach)
  4. Both reduce to coverage by a structured set on a circle
  5. The 'triple' structure: LRC has {LS, LL, SL} fiber triples;
     twin-Goldbach has {6k-2, 6k, 6k+2} exception triples

